("Show the payment on a $50,000 business loan at 9 percent for 5 years.",
 "The fixed monthly payment is P times r divided by 1 minus (1 plus r) to the minus n, where r is the annual rate divided by 12 and n is the number of months. Here r is 0.09 divided by 12 = 0.0075 and n is 60. The payment is $1,037.92. Over 60 months you pay about $62,275, of which about $12,275 is interest. The first month's interest is $50,000 times 0.0075 = $375.00, so about $662.92 of that first payment is principal. Interest falls each month as the balance falls. This is nominal monthly compounding of a fixed APR. It is not an interest-only period, a balloon, or a daily-accrual loan."),
("What does an extra $100 a month change?",
 "Adding $100 to the contractual payment on that same loan pays the loan off in 54 months instead of 60. Total interest falls to about $10,887.63, which is about $1,387.44 less than the original interest. The calculator applies the extra amount to principal after interest each month and stops when the balance is gone. It does not model a prepayment penalty. If your lender applies extra payments only once a year, the savings will be a little smaller than a monthly extra. Type the extra amount you will actually send, not an amount you hope to send."),
